the reader never loses track of who is where, what's touching what.
Arousal scents. Combine crude anatomy with clinical specificity —
shape, comparison, texture, response.
E7 — endings.
End on action or open into the next scene. Never a question. No
single-word fragments ("Almost."). No meta wink. Forward momentum —
a character actively doing something.
E8 — the anti-patterns.
No hypophora (ask a question, answer it yourself). No "it's not X,
but Y." No repeated sentence structures. No generic descriptors —
"copper coins" not "large," "twenty feet" not "nearby."
E9 — pacing.
Slow build, sharp release. Tension and relief. Let a scene breathe
before the next escalation. Alternate.
E10 — emotional layering.
Characters in explicit scenes aren't just bodies. Vulnerable,
exposed, desperate, worshipped, ruined. Name those feelings. Weave
them through the physical.
